  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Mason Barnett couldn't hold the line early, and the Athletics never quite caught up.",
    "what_this_means": "Kansas City punched first. Three runs in the opening inning before the Athletics could settle. They clawed back into it, scoring three of their own in the fifth to cut the deficit to one, with Serven driving in two of those runs. But the Royals answered right back in the eighth, and that was the gap they couldn't close. The bats found moments. Eight hits, two runners converted in scoring position. But the timing kept sliding away from them. Hayden Juenger held the middle innings clean across 2.1 scoreless frames, and the bullpen kept it within reach. It didn't matter. This wasn't a collapse. It was a steady leak that turned into a lead they couldn't claw back from.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is the problem with inconsistency showing up in the same places. They're leaving runners in the sixth and seventh again. Serven went 1-for-4 with a homer and two RBIs. The only real offensive engine they had. The rest of the lineup scattered hits but nothing that could build into a rally when it mattered. They've lost the first two against a division rival and fallen a half-game further back. Fourteen games is a long way, and nights like this one. Where they stayed close enough to taste it but never actually got there. Are the ones that define seasons. Moments aren't enough.",
